    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" xml:lang="lzh" xml:space="preserve">
<ab>
<lb n="1"/><persName>安公</persName>
<lb n="2"/>之碑
<lb n="1"/><persName>大沙門僧安</persName>，不安所安，安所不
<lb n="2"/>能安。大道一，不一所一，一所不
<lb n="3"/>能一。不安所安，不安扵安。安所
<lb n="4"/>不安，能安扵安。不一所一，不一
<lb n="5"/>其一。一所不一，能一其一。詞曰：
<lb n="6"/>安故能一，一故能安。安一，一安。
<lb n="7"/>巖上雕刊
<lb n="8"/><supplied reason="lost">雙</supplied>林後千六百廿秊。
</ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus"/>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en">
                <p>The Stele of Sire SerenityThe Great Renunciant (śramaṇa) “Monk Serenity”: [that is to say, he] does not consider serene that which has been made serene, but considers serene that which cannot be made serene. “Great Way of Unity”: [that is to say, he] does not consider as unified that which has been unified, but considers as unified that which cannot be unified. “He does not consider serene that which has been made serene”— he does not find serenity in serenity. “He considers serene that which cannot be made serene”— he is able to be serene in serenity. “He does not consider as unified that which has been unified”— he does not consider that unity as unity. “He considers as unified that which is not unified”— he is able to consider that unity as unity.The encomium says: He is serene, thus he is able to unify; he sees unity, thus he is able to make things serene. Serene, thus unity; unity, thus serene. Carved on the cliff one thousand six hundred and twenty years after [Śākyamuni entered into nirvana under] the twin trees [553].</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
